---
import LegalShell from "../../components/legal/LegalShell.astro";
import { PRIVACY_POLICY_VERSION, SMS_SUPPORT_EMAIL } from "../../lib/smsProgram";
---

<LegalShell
  title="Privacy and Data Processing Policy — Binah"
  description="Binah's privacy and personal data processing policy, including the platform, WhatsApp, email, SMS, artificial intelligence, and website analytics."
  eyebrow="Legal document"
  heading="Privacy and Data Processing Policy"
  canonicalPath="/en/privacy"
  alternatePath="/privacidad"
  version={PRIVACY_POLICY_VERSION}
  effectiveDate="September 8, 2026"
  locale="en"
>
  <article id="english" lang="en" aria-labelledby="privacy-en-title">
    <div class="legal-language-heading">
      <span class="mono">EN</span>
      <h2 id="privacy-en-title">Privacy and Personal Data Processing Policy</h2>
    </div>

    <section>
      <h3>1. Controller identity and contact information</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Soluciones ALCO S.A.S.</strong> ("Soluciones ALCO," "Binah," "we," "us") operates the Binah brand, website, and platform. When it determines the purposes and means of processing, it is the data Controller under Colombia's Law 1581 of 2012 and its implementing regulations.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Principal place of business: Barranquilla, Atlántico, Colombia.</li>
        <li>Electronic address and website: <a href="https://binah.co">https://binah.co</a>.</li>
        <li>Privacy contact: Privacy and Support Team.</li>
        <li>Email: <a href={`mailto:${SMS_SUPPORT_EMAIL}`}>{SMS_SUPPORT_EMAIL}</a>.</li>
        <li>Contact telephone and WhatsApp: <a href="tel:+573147040528">+57 314 704 0528</a>.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h3>2. Scope and Binah's roles</h3>
      <p>
        This Policy applies to binah.co, the Binah platform, forms, communications, connected channels, integrations, support, and related services.
      </p>
      <p>
        Binah acts as a <strong>Controller</strong> for data about website visitors, prospects, business customers, platform users, vendors, and people who communicate directly with Binah for our own purposes, such as sales, contracting, billing, support, service protection, and legal compliance.
      </p>
      <p>
        When a customer organization uploads contacts or connects channels to receive, store, analyze, or send messages involving its own customers, prospects, employees, or other people, that organization normally determines why the information is used and acts as the <strong>Controller</strong>. Soluciones ALCO acts as a <strong>Processor</strong> and processes the data on its behalf, according to its instructions, service configuration, and applicable agreement. Each customer organization is responsible for the authorizations, notices, and other grounds required for its processing and communications.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you receive a message from an organization that uses Binah, you should first direct privacy questions or requests to that organization. You may also contact us, and we will assist the relevant Controller without limiting your rights.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h3>3. Sources of information</h3>
      <p>We may receive information:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Directly from you when you visit the website, submit a form, create an account, or communicate with Binah or a customer organization.</li>
        <li>From business customers and authorized users who upload, create, import, or synchronize contacts and operational data.</li>
        <li>From channel and infrastructure providers, such as WhatsApp/Meta and messaging, email, authentication, storage, or telephony providers.</li>
        <li>From integrations a customer organization chooses to connect, such as e-commerce, payment, invoicing, logistics, or other business tools.</li>
        <li>Automatically through technical logs, cookies, similar technologies, and delivery or interaction events.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h3>4. Information we process</h3>
      <p>Depending on the relationship, enabled modules, and interaction, we may process:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Identity, contact, and account data:</strong> name, identity or tax document when needed, company, title, email, telephone, address, city, country, credentials, and organization role.</li>
        <li><strong>Channel profiles and destinations:</strong> WhatsApp number and identifiers, profile name or username, profile picture when supplied by the channel, email addresses, senders, recipients, and connected business accounts.</li>
        <li><strong>Communication content:</strong> text, subject, replies, notes, files, images, audio, voice notes, video, documents, locations, shared contacts, and interactions with catalogs or buttons.</li>
        <li><strong>Metadata and events:</strong> date and time, internal and provider identifiers, channel, participants, send, delivery, read, or failure status, replies, blocks and, for email, opens, clicks, bounces, complaints, and unsubscribes when tracking is enabled.</li>
        <li><strong>Commercial and operational data:</strong> CRM history, preferences, labels, opportunities, orders, products, payments, invoices, delivery addresses, logistics, support, tasks, and campaign relationships.</li>
        <li><strong>Consent and preferences:</strong> authorization or withdrawal, source, date, policy text or version, exclusion lists, blocks, suppressions, and compliance evidence.</li>
        <li><strong>Technical and usage data:</strong> IP address, browser, device, user agent, pages and features used, cookies, and access, security, audit, and diagnostic logs.</li>
        <li><strong>Automation and AI outputs:</strong> transcripts, summaries, intent, category, sentiment, labels, extracted fields, drafts, responses, and records of actions taken.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Free-form message or file content may include sensitive information or data about children if the sender chooses to include it. Review section 16 before providing such information.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h3>5. Processing purposes</h3>
      <p>As applicable, we process information to:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Create and administer accounts, organizations, permissions, subscriptions, billing, and support.</li>
        <li>Provide the omnichannel inbox, retain history, connect contacts and conversations, assign owners, and support search, reports, and follow-up.</li>
        <li>Receive and send requested messages, templates, files, transactional notifications, reminders, and service communications.</li>
        <li>Run automations and AI features authorized by the customer organization, such as classification, summarization, transcription, extraction, recommendations, response generation, and escalation.</li>
        <li>Manage campaigns, segmentation, measurement, attribution, and exclusions only when the required authorization or other lawful ground exists.</li>
        <li>Connect channels to orders, inventory, payments, invoicing, logistics, support, and other processes configured by the customer organization.</li>
        <li>Analyze website and product use, measure Binah's own campaigns, and improve experience, reliability, and performance.</li>
        <li>Prevent fraud, spam, abuse, unauthorized access, and incidents; debug errors; and maintain security and audit records.</li>
        <li>Meet legal, regulatory, accounting, tax, and contractual duties, respond to valid requests, and establish or defend rights.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="whatsapp-privacy">
      <h3>6. WhatsApp and other messaging channels</h3>
      <p>
        When you or a customer organization communicates through WhatsApp, Binah may receive, display, store, and transmit the WhatsApp number or identifier, visible profile information, content and attachments, reply context, template, catalog, or advertising interactions, and send, delivery, or read status. We use this data to operate the conversation, associate it with the correct contact, provide support, automate responses, maintain traceability, and carry out the customer organization's instructions.
      </p>
      <p>
        Messages also pass through WhatsApp/Meta and technical messaging providers connected to the service. Those third parties process data under their own terms and policies and may retain records in their infrastructure. Binah does not control independent processing by a recipient or an external platform outside the services we provide.
      </p>
      <p>
        Operational or transactional communications — such as responding to a request, confirming an order, providing delivery information, or offering support — are distinct from advertising. WhatsApp marketing campaigns or messages must have the required authorization and respect purpose, authorized channels, preferences, hours, and applicable rules. You may ask the sender at any time to stop advertising messages. For messages sent directly by Binah, send <strong>STOP</strong> or <strong>NO MÁS</strong> through WhatsApp or request an opt-out at <a href={`mailto:${SMS_SUPPORT_EMAIL}`}>{SMS_SUPPORT_EMAIL}</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        WhatsApp authorization is not email or SMS authorization. Likewise, a purchase, prior conversation, or inclusion on an imported list does not by itself authorize advertising through a different channel.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="email-privacy">
      <h3>7. Email</h3>
      <p>
        For inbound or outbound email, we may process sender and recipient names and addresses, subject, text or HTML body, replies, attachments, headers and authentication data, provider identifiers, and dates. We may also turn an email into a contact, opportunity, task, or conversation and extract relevant fields under an automation configured by the customer organization.
      </p>
      <p>
        When an organization enables measurement, we receive events such as acceptance, delivery, open, click —including the associated URL—, bounce, complaint, failure, or unsubscribe. We use these events to measure delivery, prevent further sends to suppressed addresses, investigate abuse, and produce statistics. Open indicators may be inaccurate because of privacy protections or email client behavior.
      </p>
      <p>
        Advertising email sent directly by Binah will include or provide an easy opt-out mechanism when required. You may also request an opt-out at <a href={`mailto:${SMS_SUPPORT_EMAIL}`}>{SMS_SUPPORT_EMAIL}</a>. A marketing opt-out does not prevent communications strictly necessary for a contract, security, billing, responding to a request, or providing a service.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="sms-privacy">
      <h3>8. SMS program privacy</h3>
      <p>
        Binah sends SMS Marketing Program messages only to people who provided express, separate, and verifiable consent. We may retain the mobile number, consent status and time, accepted text and version, source URL, user agent, IP address when technically and legally appropriate, keywords, and later help or withdrawal records.
      </p>
      <p>
        Mobile information and SMS consent records are not sold, rented, or disclosed to third parties for their own marketing or promotional purposes. Limited infrastructure providers, including carriers, aggregators, and technical platforms, may process what is necessary solely to deliver messages, process STOP, respond to HELP, prevent fraud, maintain compliance evidence, and meet carrier or legal requirements.
      </p>
      <p>
        Reply <strong>STOP</strong> to any SMS to opt out or <strong>HELP</strong> for assistance. You may also email us. An SMS withdrawal does not automatically withdraw separate WhatsApp or email authorizations, and vice versa.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h3>9. Artificial intelligence and automated decisions</h3>
      <p>
        If a customer organization enables AI features, Binah may send relevant portions of messages, files, audio, history, and business context to model providers to transcribe, classify, summarize, extract data, generate drafts or responses, and run configured tools. We retain outputs and operational records when needed to display history, audit actions, improve security, or provide the service.
      </p>
      <p>
        The customer organization chooses which agents, instructions, sources, and automations to enable and must implement human review when appropriate. AI outputs may be inaccurate. Binah does not use a customer organization's conversation content on its own behalf to make decisions that produce legal effects for message recipients.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h3>10. Authorization, sensitive data, and permitted grounds</h3>
      <p>
        We obtain prior, express, and informed authorization when required by law and retain evidence in a form that can be consulted. We may also process data when needed to perform a requested relationship, meet a legal or contractual duty, respond to a valid order, or in other circumstances excepted by law. Silence is not authorization.
      </p>
      <p>
        Answering questions about sensitive data is optional. Do not provide sensitive data to Binah or configure its collection through the platform unless it is appropriate and necessary for a legitimate purpose and explicit consent or another applicable legal authorization exists.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="cookies-analytics">
      <h3>11. Cookies, analytics, and digital advertising</h3>
      <p>
        The public website may use local storage, cookies, and similar technologies. Some are necessary to remember preferences or maintain features; others help us understand visits and measure campaigns. Commercial pages currently may load Google Analytics/Google Tag and Meta Pixel, which receive information such as IP address, device, browser, page visited, referring URL, and identifiers associated with their technologies.
      </p>
      <p>
        Google and Meta may process this information under their own policies and act as independent controllers for some purposes. You can block or delete cookies through your browser and use controls those providers offer; doing so may limit some functions or measurements. Binah's legal pages and public SMS consent form disable Binah's marketing tags.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h3>12. Recipients and service providers</h3>
      <p>We may provide limited access or disclose data to:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Authorized Binah personnel and customer-organization users, according to their roles and permissions.</li>
        <li>Cloud, database, storage, security, authentication, analytics, support, AI, messaging, WhatsApp, email, SMS, telephony, file-processing, and content-delivery providers.</li>
        <li>Integrations and partners a customer organization chooses to connect or instructs us to send information to.</li>
        <li>Authorities, courts, regulators, or other parties when required by law, a valid order, the need to protect rights, or a business transaction subject to appropriate safeguards.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Providers that may be involved, depending on module and configuration, include Convex, WorkOS, Meta/WhatsApp, Infobip, Sinch, Mailgun, OpenAI, object-storage providers, Google —including Google Analytics—, and Meta —including Meta Pixel—. We require providers acting on our behalf to follow confidentiality, security, and purpose-limitation obligations appropriate to the service.
      </p>
      <p>
        We do not sell personal data or authorize our processors to use conversation content, contacts, or consent records for the processors' own marketing. A customer organization retains control over data processed through its account, subject to its agreement and the law.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h3>13. International processing and transfers</h3>
      <p>
        Some providers and services may process data inside or outside Colombia and in regions selected by Binah or the customer organization. When processing is a transmission on behalf of a Controller, the relevant instructions and data-processing agreements apply. For transfers and other international processing, we use the authorizations, exceptions, agreements, and safeguards required to pursue an appropriate level of protection.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h3>14. Retention, deletion, and anonymization</h3>
      <p>
        Databases remain active while the operational, contractual, commercial, or legal purposes described continue. We retain each category only for a reasonable and necessary period based on the relationship, customer-organization instructions, retention configuration, and administrative, accounting, tax, security, audit, or legal-claims obligations.
      </p>
      <p>
        Content and attachments may be retained to preserve the history requested by the customer organization. Temporary files and technical logs may have different periods. Evidence of consent, withdrawal, blocking, deletion, delivery, and exclusion lists may be kept after an opt-out to demonstrate compliance and prevent further sends. When identifiable data is no longer required, we take reasonable steps to delete, unlink, or anonymize it unless a legal or contractual obligation requires retention.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h3>15. Security and incidents</h3>
      <p>
        We use reasonable administrative, technical, and organizational measures, such as access and permission controls, organization separation, encrypted transport, audit logs, vendor management, backups, and security monitoring. No system is completely secure. We will investigate incidents and provide notices or cooperation required by applicable law and agreements.
      </p>
      <p>
        Users must protect their credentials, limit internal permissions, and avoid sending unnecessary information through channels they do not control.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h3>16. Children</h3>
      <p>
        Binah's business services are not directed to children. Customer organizations must not intentionally use Binah to process children's data unless the processing serves and respects the child's best interests, protects fundamental rights, and authorization is obtained from the representative after hearing the child according to maturity, or another legal ground applies. Contact us if you believe we received such data improperly.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="privacy-rights">
      <h3>17. Rights and procedures for inquiries and complaints</h3>
      <p>Subject to applicable law, a data subject may:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Know, access, update, correct, and request deletion of personal data.</li>
        <li>Request proof of authorization, subject to legal exceptions, and learn how information has been used.</li>
        <li>Withdraw authorization where permitted and withdraw channel-specific marketing consent.</li>
        <li>Access personal data free of charge where provided by law.</li>
        <li>File a complaint with Colombia's Superintendence of Industry and Commerce after completing the inquiry or complaint process with the Controller or Processor.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The data subject, successors, representative, or attorney may send a request to <a href={`mailto:${SMS_SUPPORT_EMAIL}`}>{SMS_SUPPORT_EMAIL}</a> with the subject <strong>Personal data request</strong>. Include the name, response method, relationship with Binah or the customer organization, right being exercised, a clear description of the facts or data, and supporting documents. We will request only information reasonably needed to verify identity, authority, and scope.
      </p>
      <p>
        Under Colombian law, inquiries will be answered within ten (10) business days after receipt. If more time is needed, we will explain why and provide a date no more than five (5) additional business days later. Complete complaints will be answered within fifteen (15) business days. If more time is needed, we will explain why and provide a date no more than eight (8) additional business days later. If a complaint is incomplete, we will request correction within five (5) days; if it is not completed within two (2) months after that request, it will be treated as withdrawn. If we are not competent to resolve a request, we will forward it to the appropriate party when possible and inform the requester.
      </p>
      <p>
        A deletion or withdrawal request does not apply when a legal or contractual duty requires retention. Withdrawal does not affect processing that was lawful before it was received.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h3>18. Changes and effective period</h3>
      <p>
        This version takes effect on <strong>September 8, 2026</strong> and replaces the prior version. We may update it to reflect operational, technical, or legal changes. We will publish the current version on this page and provide timely and effective notice of material changes before implementation when required by law. If a change affects a purpose requiring new authorization, we will request it before that processing begins.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</LegalShell>
